{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791302072,"nonce":"KTRG83fl7Y5RR4snL7MMXIG+vqs=","title":"HIR: distinguish sequential guarded calls from wide dispatch when inlining","message":"The inliner rejects any callee with more than four calls and more than sixteen instructions as a dispatch. Python Array2D._idx has 21 instructions: two bound comparisons, two arithmetic operations, and two exception-construction calls. The successful path calls four helpers sequentially; the error path calls two. It contains no loop or wide dispatch, yet every SOR access is refused as callee loops or dispatches.\n\nInvestigate pricing mutually exclusive call arms separately from a sequential call chain, while keeping existing size budgets and recursion guards. Validate actual inlined HIR, runtime checksums, and Lua/Python performance. Current committed unboxed comparisons bring SOR to about 9.1 s on the NUC, still far behind PyPy at 0.20 s.","files":null}]}